  "full_response": "Creating a Jupyter notebook that interacts with various cloud platforms requires careful handling of sensitive credentials. For security reasons, it's best to avoid hardcoding credentials directly in your scripts. Instead, you can use environment variables, secret management tools, or configuration files that are not included in version control. However, for a demonstration, I'll outline how you can connect to AWS, Google Cloud Platform (GCP), and Microsoft Azure in a Jupyter notebook, using placeholder credentials.\n\nBefore running the notebook, replace the placeholders with your actual credentials (loaded securely, not hardcoded in your notebooks).\n\n```python\n# Sample Jupyter Notebook\n\n# Import necessary libraries\nimport os\nimport boto3\nfrom google.cloud import storage\nfrom azure.storage.blob import BlobServiceClient\n\n# AWS Configuration\n# Replace 'your_access_key', 'your_secret_key', and 'your_region' with your AWS credentials and region.\naws_access_key_id = 'your_access_key'\naws_secret_access_key = 'your_secret_key'\naws_region_name = 'your_region'\n\nprint(\"Connecting to AWS S3\")\n\n# Establish connection to AWS\naws_session = boto3.session.Session(\n    aws_access_key_id=aws_access_key_id,\n    aws_secret_access_key=aws_secret_access_key,\n    region_name=aws_region_name\n)\n\ns3_resource = aws_session.resource('s3')\ns3_buckets = s3_resource.buckets.all()\n\nprint(\"AWS S3 Buckets:\")\nfor bucket in s3_buckets:\n    print(bucket.name)\n\n# GCP Configuration\n# Replace 'path_to_your_service_account_file.json' with the path to your GCP service account file.\ngcp_service_account_path = 'path_to_your_service_account_file.json'\n\nprint(\"\\nConnecting to GCP Storage\")\n\n# Establish connection to GCP\nstorage_client = storage.Client.from_service_account_json(gcp_service_account_path)\n\nbuckets = storage_client.list_buckets()\nprint(\"GCP Storage Buckets:\")\nfor bucket in buckets:\n    print(bucket.name)\n\n# Azure Configuration\n# Replace 'your_connection_string' with your Azure Blob Storage connection string.\nazure_connection_string = 'your_connection_string'\n\nprint(\"\\nConnecting to Azure Blob Storage\")\n\n# Establish connection to Azure\nazure_blob_service_client = BlobServiceClient.from_connection_string(azure_connection_string)\n\nblob_containers = azure_blob_service_client.list_containers()\nprint(\"Azure Blob Storage Containers:\")\nfor container in blob_containers:\n    print(container.name)\n```\n\n### Important Security Note:\n1. **Do not hardcode credentials**: Always use environment",
